/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A potentiometer has a resistive track, two end terminals and a wiper that slides on the track.
   - Connect the track ends to a supply Vs (V).
   - On a wire-wound track the wiper jumps from turn to turn.
   - The straight law assumes no current leaves the wiper.
   - The extra term k = α(1 − α)·r is zero at α = 0 and α = 1.
   - The error is a fraction k/(1 + k) of the ideal reading.
   - Linearity is good on a uniform film track, but wire-wound tracks are only linear on average; each turn is a step.
   - Use a potentiometer where a cheap, absolute position signal is enough.
*/
