/* book exam traps: Quoting Vs·x/L as the real output while a load is connected. The divider is linear only when RL is much larger than R; use the loaded formula whenever the load is within about ten times the track. | Mixing x and α. The formula Vs·x/L needs x and L in the same unit; α = x/L is dimensionless. Putting 50 instead of 0.5 into an α formula gives an answer 100 times too large. | Saying loading error is largest at the ends. It is zero at x = 0 and x = L. As a fraction of the reading it is largest at mid-stroke, where α(1 − α) peaks; in volts it peaks a little later, near two-thirds of the stroke, at about 0.15·r·Vs for a light load. | Confusing resolution with accuracy. A wire-wound pot has resolution L/N from turn spacing; a film pot can resolve finer but still drifts with temperature, wear and contact noise. | Reporting loading error on the wrong base. State whether the percentage is of the ideal reading or of full scale; at mid-stroke with RL = R they are 20 % and 10 %. */
