/* book exam traps: Dropping the 10⁻⁶: 500 µε is 500 × 10⁻⁶, not 500. A strain of 500 would be a 50,000 % length change, impossible for elastic metal. | Mixing ε and ΔL/L: ε is ΔL/L, so a 0.5 mm stretch on a 1 m bar is 500 µε, not 0.5 µε. | Treating GF as a resistance: GF is dimensionless. The same GF gives ΔR = GF·ε·R, so a 350 Ω gauge gives nearly three times the ΔR of a 120 Ω gauge at the same strain. | Assuming all semiconductor gauges have positive ΔR: doping type and crystal direction set the sign. Check the datasheet before wiring a bridge. | Forgetting transverse sensitivity and lead resistance in a rosette or quarter bridge: both shift the reading even when the axial strain is correct. */
